From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: sadatdaniel <sadatdaniel@yahoo.com>
Date: Thu, 4 Sep 2026 00:20:00 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] f_conn_gadget: guard against double misc_register

conn_gadget_device is a single file-static struct miscdevice, and
conn_gadget_setup() calls misc_register() on it unconditionally, with no
check for an existing registration. It also overwrites the global
_conn_gadget_dev before doing so, which orphans the previous object's kref
and means conn_gadget_cleanup() - the only caller of misc_deregister() -
can never run for it.

Instantiate the conn_gadget configfs function a second time and the result
is memory corruption, not an error:

  misc_register() begins with INIT_LIST_HEAD(&misc->list), so the static
  node is reset to point at itself while the neighbours already linked to
  it in misc_list still point at it. misc_list becomes circular. The next
  list_for_each_entry(c, &misc_list, list) inside misc_open() never
  terminates - and it runs inside that function's misc_mtx critical
  section, so the spinning task holds the global misc mutex forever, at
  100% CPU, in uninterruptible kernel context.

Every subsequent open() of any misc device on the system then blocks:
/dev/mali0, /dev/ion, /dev/binder, /dev/hwbinder, /dev/uinput, the
compositor and every Android HAL. Observed on this device as 94 tasks
parked at misc_open+0x34 with load average 115, and it presents as "the
GPU is hung" rather than as anything USB-related.

It is reached in practice because usb_moded (the GNU/Linux side) and
Android's vendor_init (inside the container) both drive USB gadget
configfs. f_mass_storage survives the same race because device_create()
fails it loudly with -EEXIST; the Samsung gadget functions have no such
check and corrupt the list silently instead.

Diagnosis in a50-ubuntu-touch docs/experiments/006-what-we-missed.md,
including why the spinning holder is invisible to D-state sweeps, wchan
grouping, SysRq-T and fd inspection alike.

kref_get_unless_zero() rather than kref_get() so a concurrent teardown
that has already dropped the count to zero falls through to a full setup
instead of resurrecting a dying object; it is the idiom this file already
uses in conn_gadget_open().

Verified to apply cleanly against the pinned tree with `git apply --check`.
NOT YET BOOT-TESTED - see README.md in this directory. The workaround
deployed instead keeps the container away from USB gadget configfs
entirely (a50-ubuntu-touch scripts/apply-device-workarounds.sh).
---
diff --git a/drivers/usb/gadget/function/f_conn_gadget.c b/drivers/usb/gadget/function/f_conn_gadget.c
index ed3db0865..e98af7f3c 100644
--- a/drivers/usb/gadget/function/f_conn_gadget.c
+++ b/drivers/usb/gadget/function/f_conn_gadget.c
@@ -1227,6 +1227,19 @@ static int conn_gadget_setup(struct conn_gadget_instance *fi_conn_gadget)
 
 	printk(KERN_INFO "conn_gadget_setup\n");
 
+	/*
+	 * conn_gadget_device is a single static miscdevice. Registering it
+	 * twice makes misc_list circular (misc_register() starts with
+	 * INIT_LIST_HEAD on a node still linked into that list), which hangs
+	 * every misc-device open on the system inside misc_open(), holding
+	 * misc_mtx forever. Reuse the existing instance instead.
+	 */
+	if (_conn_gadget_dev &&
+	    kref_get_unless_zero(&_conn_gadget_dev->kref)) {
+		printk(KERN_INFO "conn_gadget_setup: already registered\n");
+		return 0;
+	}
+
 	dev = kzalloc(sizeof(*dev), GFP_KERNEL);
 	if (!dev) {
 		printk(KERN_ERR "alloc conn_gadget_dev F\n");
--
